    (4, None, '\'The stove will return to the kitchen on time, Lyova.\' "Vernyotsya" is "will come back", with -sya on the end. "Vovremya", "on time", is written as one word. "Na kukhnyu" is "to the kitchen": "kukhnya" changes to "kukhnyu".'),
    (5, None, '\'Do not say later that I do not listen.\' "Ne govori potom" is "don\'t say later". "Slushayu" is "I listen", the same word as in "ya slushayu", "I\'m listening". Here it means listening and doing what you\'re told.'),
    (6, None, '\'I am simply worried about the evening service.\' "Perezhivayu za" is "I\'m worried about": the verb "perezhivat" takes "za". "Vecherny", "evening", comes from "vecher". "Servis" is borrowed and easy to catch.'),
    (7, None, '\'Tonight our restaurant will make a fortune.\' "Zarabotaet" is "will earn", from "rabota", "work". "Sostoyanie" means "a fortune" here, and it also means "state" or "condition". "Segodnya vecherom" is "this evening".'),
    (8, None, '\'All right, chef, I will get back to work.\' "Ladno" is the easy "all right". "Vernus k rabote" is "I\'ll return to work": after "k", "rabota" becomes "rabote". Lyova goes back to work.'),
    (9, None, '\'Go and supervise the hot kitchen section.\' "Prosledi za" is "keep an eye on". "Tsekh" is a workshop, and "goryachiy tsekh", "hot workshop", is the part of the kitchen where the cooking happens. "Idi" is "go", to one person.'),
